I finally managed it.  I had a previous wizard leave the amulet behind
when leaving the dungeon, and another get his tail kicked on Astral.

My first ascension was a valkyrie.  Not for the usual reasons; I just
kept playing them because I got a kick out of the hammer.  That was
followed by Sir Hawk, out to gain vengeance on Lugh.

A number of things never appeared in the game, or appeared very late.

The only magical helmet to ever appear was a dunce cap.  Boots were
limited to water walking, fumbling, and jumping.  No wands of
polymporph, only two potions, and the spellbook appeared in the sanctum,
but was burned up almost immediately.  No spellbook or ring of 
levitation, either.  Or scrolls of genocide.  Or rings of slow digestion
or hunger.

I started with a marker, and would find another one early in Hell.  This
provided a few enchant armor and the spellbook of teleport away. They
also helped charge the ring of protection.

My initial pet stayed around unusually long.  It went on the quest with
me, and I think it started its way through Hell before hitting the level
teleporter and returning to L1, where I'd find it on the way out (or
maybe it hit one on one of my trips back to the stash).   I took him
from the dungeon with me, along with a purple worm (the other was
eating), a couple of dragons, 3 olog hai, and a winged gargoyle.  I
believe they all made it to the plain of water.  Noctus, my cat, finally
drowned there.   Knowing what I know now, I suppose I should have
whistled him to an air bubble, but . . .


Early on, I lost a chunk of gold when sitting on a throne.  Only then
did I realize that I really had no reason to sit on thrones, as I'd turn
down the wish or genocide . . .

A couple of dicey moments as hit points went dangerously low, but I
managed to escape these.

I cancelled the daggers from the mines, and produced 39 or so +6
daggers.  I don't think I ever had more than 12 in inventory, and only
tossed them a couple of times. 

At the castle, I blasted the gate and let my pets run amok--there had
not been a single instrument to that point, and I wouldn't find the
water walking boots until later.  I picked up red dragon armor, but
didn't even go to the side portions where the wand was contained.  I
used light & jumping to get through to the dragons.

I then took the pets up two levels to a maze, and lured all the wraiths
in the valley back above the castle.  I brought up a couple at a time,
and every time I had several and they started getting in the way when
I'd head back down, I'd take them upstairs.  They accounted for seven or
so levels.

Hell in general was its usual boring self.  There was that scary moment,
though, before taking out the wizard, when I headed back to my stash.
Having accepted that I wouldn't find a helmet, I used A to take off all
of my armor except the helmet, and enchanted it.  Or so I thought.  I'd
seen a bloody grid bug and stopped removing armor, costing me my +5
speed boots (the only that appeared in the game).

Just before opening the sanctum, I alchemized the healing potions.  They
were bizarrely common, filling out for all of the important things that
never showed.  I believe that I quaffed 38 of them, before noticing
another 18 extra healing to promote.  I think there were another dozen
or so before I left the dungeon, putting me at over 700 points for the
end game.

The sanctum proved to be annoying.  I managed to set a couple of worms
lose, as well as a couple of dragons, and tamed a few more of what were
down there.  Rodney beat me to the high priest by a few steps, and he
and his twin hung out at the upstairs before deciding to leave (I not
only didn't realize that he could do that with the amulet, but also that
he'd go up when I was nowhere nearby).

I spent a very long time looking for it before heading back up and
taking it from him.

Spells began wearing out at inconvenient time.  It's always seemed
unsporting to write down turn numbers for reading spellbooks, but I had
several trips over the game when critical spells ran out.  I was never
able to renew the fireball spell; I have no idea what happened to the
book.  [hmm, now it occurs to me that it could have gone up or down a
level from my stash, but I don't think the books were left exposed until
long after it was missing.]

I spent most of my post-castle time burdened, including most of the
Astral plane.  I just couldn't decide what to leave behind, and assumed
that my alchemy would eventually reduce the burden--but was wrong.

The mysterious force was more annoying than usual, but at least I
eventually remembered to put down the amulet before the first trip
upstairs as I transported my herd.  Once in the main dungeon, I started
trimming the useless pets out, knowing I could only leave with 8.

The archons I would meet were annoying.  None of my pets would slug it
out with them, leaving me to prick them with magicbane (except for the
couple who didn't have shields).

I seemed to use a food nearly every level I climbed, and began worrying
about my ultimate supply.

Eventually I was able to leave the dungeon, after refreshing about 8
spells and stashing "teleport away" in my bag.

Earth was fairly fast, and I discharged a couple of wands to get to the
very close portal.

I used the first of my potions of levitation to get through air, which
is where the archons got to be a pain.  I also had to be careful about
spellcasting, as I couldn't drop the amulet without using up another
potion to retrieve it.  I suppose I should keep a bullwhip next time.
I think this was also where I discovered that elementals rarely go
through the portal when whistled.

Fire produced a few more pets, and I made sure I was at full energy
before hitting water.

Oh, yes.  Water.  And an accidentally tamed gremlin.  I had a couple
dozen pet gremlins for a while.  But first, I was swallowed, and
foolishly dropped the amulet to kill it.  I realized this when I went to
put it on to help find the portal . . .

The game had not provided any amulets of magical breathing.  Nor had it
provided any oilskin items.  (there was apparently no gate to ludios).

It quickly became apparent that detect treasure wouldn't be much help,
as it showed no amulets.  I no longer had a crystal ball, either--I'd
left the dungeon with one that had two charges, and with two scrolls of
find gold.  I used the ball on the first two planes and ditched it, and
would find a third scroll along the way.

I decided that the archon must have the amulet, as he was hiding on the
other edge of the map.  Turns out that he didn't, and I had to run
about, hoping that I didn't run out of levitation, checking every pile.
I had learned from wizard mode that it would float when a monster
dropped it (so it's entirely possible that it was floating the whole
time to start with, I suppose, as it would have been inside the vortex).
I found it, and still had a couple of potions, but not many pets.  Only
one useful dragon and a couple of elementals.  

I used my last scroll of gold detection to find out which direction to
head, but was interrupted again on the way over, and had to use the
amulet for the long, slow, search--thus leaving without my dragon.

Finally at astral.  

Immediately, two arch-liches and a adventuring priestess ganged up on 
me.  No big deal, I thought.  Engrave with magicbane, drop the bag, and
zap them silly with magic missle.

What do you mean, my memory of the spell is getting weak???? This spell
is all I have that's any good!

But I managed to kill them, and flicked away a high level thief and a
couple of hostile angels with my wand.  I also tamed a few more critters
that were summoned--more olog hai and a worm, in particular.  The latter
seemed to find priests tasty . . .

I headed right to pestilince's altar, and he came to greet me
immediately.   I killed him, and he came back in just a couple of turns.
I whistled in some friends and just plain dodged him.
With a couple more teleportation zaps, and a mob of pets, I made it to
the altar, having to battle yet another Archon.  Hmm, it seems that
there were only four vanquished in the game; maybe a couple left levels?

Nope; this was the lawful altar.  *sigh*

I whistled my pets into the room; I was down to about 130 power, though
my hit points were fine.  I think this is when I quaffed the other four
potions of gain level to get to level 30.

I tried to dodge pestilence on the way out, but no such luck.  I had the
foresight to have some leaves in inventory, but I quickly had to use
that and a couple of spells.  Then it got really dicey.  Two quick hits
(I was burdened, and couldn't drop the bag and still get away with it)
left me very ill and stunned.  I pulled a couple of leaves and full
healings out of the sack, and somehow managed to survive another four or
five illnessess before being able to missle him to death (somehow, the
spell was still holding up!).

I then tried for the middle altar.  I managed to get through the first
door before having to face famine.  There were plenty of peacefull
angels by this point, but my pets were dying fast.

Between famine and something else, I kept up ending stunned and hungry.
I think I had 10 K-rations in inventory at some point on Astral; I ended
up down to 4 (but with 8 more in the bag).

He didn't stay down long, either, but I was able to make my way to the
altar--and it was my own.  [At this point I made a copy of the game,
anticipating--correctly--problems with screen and getting my screen
dumps.

I took out my remaining potions of gain energy, full healing, and gain
level, and quaffed them, puttting me at over 800 hit points.
Unfortunately, during this little binge, Death showed up with some
friends. I dropped below 500 hp at one point, I believe.

That being done, I chatted with the priest, who figured I deserved a
nice ale, whistled for my few remaing pets, thumbed my nose at
Death, and offered the Amulet.

Thoth, apparently angry over some lost pets on water, accepted the
amulet.

And so closeth the tale of hawk the wizard, who ascended while never
deigning to shift his shape, alter objects or animals, wipe out unseen
creatures, or alter the fabric of reality

